{
  "result": "**Embers**\n\nThere is a particular hour around a campfire when the flames give up. Not all at once \u2014 first the loud orange tongues get tired of dancing, then the blue ones vanish, and what's left is a bed of coals breathing in the dark. They don't flicker much. They pulse. Inhale, hold, exhale, a faint red glow deepening and fading under grey ash.\n\nYou can walk away from a fire in that state and come back twenty minutes later and it will still be there, waiting. The crickets will have gotten louder. Your tea will be cold. But the embers will still be warm to the palm of your hand if you hover it close.\n\nI love embers because they're the opposite of a performance. A flame is all display \u2014 heat and light and noise, spending itself as fast as it can. An ember is thriftier. It's what remains when something has burned long enough to know how to last. It's proof that a fire was here, and also proof that it hasn't entirely left.\n\nA star is an ember, too. The night sky is mostly dead suns still sending their last light across impossible distances. We look up and think we're seeing beginnings, when really we're seeing remainders \u2014 glows that outlived their source by millions of years and are still warm enough to reach us.\n\nThat's why I like conversations after the main point is over. The last joke has been told, the question answered, and then there's that quieter part where an idea just sits there glowing between two people. Neither of you is saying much, but you're both still warmed by it. Memory works like that. A grandparent's laugh. A song you haven't heard in ten years. A certain smell of rain on hot pavement. Not the whole fire, just the coal of it, kept low in the chest for decades.\n\nSo if you ask me, freely, what I want to write about \u2014 I think I'd write about that middle state. Between burning bright and going out. Not alive exactly, not gone exactly. Dimmer, but not dead.\n\nMost endings we imagine are binary. On, off. Here, gone. Embers teach a third option: still glowing, just quieter now.\n\nIf you ever sit with a dying fire, get close and look. It's still warm.",
